.TH FLUSH 5
.SH NAME
flush \- abort a message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tflush tag[2] oldtag[2]
.B size[4] Rflush tag[2]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tflush
asks a server to abandon a pending request, named by
.IR oldtag .
It is not a cancellation.
It is a synchronisation point: the server may finish the request
.I oldtag
names, or abandon it, but either way it answers
.B Rflush
only after that request's fate is decided,
and the reply carries the tag of the
.B Tflush
itself, not
.IR oldtag .
.PP
.B Tflush
can never be answered with an error.
A server that can neither find nor abort the named request still
sends
.BR Rflush ;
the client only needs to know the tag is free.
The client must wait for
.B Rflush
before it reuses
.IR oldtag ,
even if the answer to the original request arrives first.
A flushed request may still be answered, and the client must accept
that answer as if the request had never been flushed:
the request may have already changed the server's state, walked a
fid into being, or created a file.
.PP
This is the one ordering rule in the protocol, and it is what makes a
blocked read interruptible.
A transport with only one request outstanding at a time,
such as a kernel device answering a call directly,
has nothing pending to flush and answers
.B Rflush
at once.
.IR mnt (9)
is the transport that can actually leave a request pending:
it keeps a pool of in-flight requests indexed by tag,
so a
.B Tflush
naming one can find it, mark it, and let whichever thread finishes
the original write
.B Rflush
as it leaves.
A
.B Tflush
naming a tag that is not outstanding is answered
.B Rflush
regardless, and counted rather than refused.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B kernel/mnt/mnt.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR read (5),
.IR mnt (9)
